Project layer (discipline is perfectly nested in project, so "Economics domain" IS
"the Experimental Economics project" and the two cannot be separated in these data):
Psychology (n=68) mean d = 0.261 [0.191, 0.332], 85.3% ro > rr; Experimental Economics
(n=18) mean d = 0.266 [0.084, 0.447], 83.3% ro > rr. The two projects do not differ on
the unweighted scale (unweighted OLS difference +0.004, p = 0.96; Mann-Whitney p = 0.28).

## What is robust and what is weighting-dependent

1. **Direction is robust everywhere.** Original > replication in 82-88% of studies in
   every domain; sign share unchanged when comparing |ro| > |rr| (identical, because all
   ro > 0) and unchanged in trimmed runs (85.7-87.1%).
2. **Domains do not differ on the primary (unweighted) scale.** Unweighted HC3
   regression of d on domain: Economics intercept 0.266 (p = 0.003), Social - Economics
   -0.033 [-0.219, 0.154] p = 0.73, Cognitive - Economics +0.024 [-0.189, 0.236]
   p = 0.83. So the defensible headline is "original > replication in every domain", not
   "only in these domains".
3. **Domains DO differ once precision is taken into account (equally prominent
   sensitivity).** Inverse-variance weighted: Social 0.208 [0.161,0.255] and Cognitive
   0.206 [0.132,0.279] vs Economics 0.013 [-0.052, 0.078]; meta-regression contrasts
   Social - Economics +0.195 [+0.086, 0.304] p = 0.0006, Cognitive - Economics
   +0.193 [+0.060, 0.325] p = 0.0050, joint discipline Wald F(2) = 7.03 p = 0.0015.
4. **The Economics divergence is not an artifact of tiny originals, but it is an
   artifact of precision.** Of the 5 studies with no < 10, only 2 are Experimental
   Economics (Chen & Chen 2011, Duffy & Puzzello 2014; the other 3 are Cognitive).
   Dropping all no < 10 and |ro| < 0.2 studies (n = 75; EE n = 14) leaves the unweighted
   Economics gap intact: mean d = 0.190 [0.063, 0.316], 85.7% ro > rr, paired p = 0.0065
   (Social 0.240, Cognitive 0.311). But within EE the gap shrinks systematically with
   sample size (corr(d, se_fisr) = +0.65; EE studies in the high replication-N half
   mean d = 0.096 vs 0.436 in the low-N half), which is why the EE precision-weighted
   estimate is ~0 in the full sample and rises only to 0.133 [0.038, 0.228] in the
   trimmed sample. Interpretation: well-powered economics replications reproduce the
   original correlations almost exactly; underpowered ones do not.
5. **Shrinkage in relative terms points at Social psychology.** Median rr/ro = 0.131 in
   Social (0.164 restricted to |ro| > 0.2) vs 0.650 Cognitive and 0.698 Economics;
   correspondingly only 20.6% of Social replications were "replicated" vs 47.1%
   Cognitive and 61.1% Economics. Social psychology loses the largest *share* of the
   original effect even though its Fisher-z drop (0.233) is not the largest.

## Caveats / scope

- 86 studies only (18 in Economics); Economics intervals are wide, and discipline is
  perfectly nested within project (Cognitive and Social exist only in the Psychology
  project), so a "domain" claim is simultaneously a "project" claim in these data.
- Post-hoc power (mean power.r 0.285 Social vs 0.726 Economics) and replication sample
  sizes (mean nr 169 Social vs 127 Economics) are descriptive context, not used as
  predictors; pm_belief (79% missing), n.r, aggregated and lab_id were not used.
- No missing or duplicated original/replication pairs; no study was excluded from the
  primary analysis (trimmed runs are sensitivity only).
